{"qid":"Q237234","names":["Kingdom of Osroene"],"title":"Kingdom of Osroene","endonym":null,"capsule":"Osroene was an Arab client kingdom centered on Edessa (modern Şanlıurfa, Turkey), ruled from c. 132 BCE by the Abgarid dynasty whose kings bore alternating names Abgar and Manu. Positioned at the crossroads of Parthian and Roman spheres, the kingdom played both powers against each other for three centuries; Abgar II was present at the Roman defeat at Carrhae (53 BCE), while Abgar VIII the Great (177–212 CE) cultivated close Roman ties and is associated with early Christian traditions claiming Edessa as one of the first Christianized royal courts. Emperor Caracalla deposed the last independent king, Abgar IX, around 214 CE, converting Osroene into a Roman province, though it remained a notable center of Syriac culture and literature long afterward.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core political history is well documented in Roman and Syriac sources, but the kingdom's exact western and southern boundaries, particularly during periods of shifting suzerainty, remain poorly mapped.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Osroene","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osroene"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Abgarid dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abgarid_dynasty"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Edessa, Mesopotamia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edessa,_Mesopotamia"}],"britannica":"place/Osroene","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Osroene","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Aryu","nativeName":"","roman":"Aryu","start":-132,"end":-127,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Abdu bar 